  "tour": {
    "title": "The Divine Council: From the Old Testament’s World to Paul",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the Bible’s categories for the unseen world, then lets the Old Testament show you its throne-room and nation-allotment assumptions before tracing that same map into the New Testament. Listen for the movement from God’s delegated heavenly administration, through rebellion and Babel, to Paul’s language of “principalities and powers” and Jesus’ lordship over them.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e4-origin-spiritual-beings-mini-qr",
        "startSec": 152,
        "endSec": 339,
        "why": "Begin here for the vocabulary. The hosts explain why “spiritual being” can be a useful way to hear Elohim, then name the group as “the Sons of Elohim,” “the heavenly host,” “the Holy Ones,” and “the Congregation or Council of God.” Most importantly, they describe these beings as “appointed delegates of how God runs the world,” giving you the basic worldview before the harder passages arrive.",
        "episodeTitle": "Origin of Spiritual Beings + Mini Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789",
        "durationSec": 4082,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:walking-talking-apocalypse",
        "startSec": 1225,
        "endSec": 1510,
        "why": "Now watch that definition become an Old Testament picture. The hosts move from Yahweh’s throne and his testing of humanity to the claim that “the skies above are often depicted as a divine throne room” containing God’s council. They connect Psalm 103 and Psalm 89 with the visions of Isaiah, Ezekiel, Daniel, and John, and explain why this matters: the throne-room scene reveals “powers behind” what appears to be happening on earth.",
        "episodeTitle": "A Walking, Talking Apocalypse",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/29823bc7-461d-4b2e-9467-a211a5ca8481/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=29823bc7-461d-4b2e-9467-a211a5ca8481",
        "durationSec": 3842,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:moses-final-words",
        "startSec": 1697,
        "endSec": 2016,
        "why": "This clip supplies the Old Testament’s national and narrative framework. The hosts work through Deuteronomy 32’s manuscript difference—“the sons of God” versus “the sons of Israel”—and read it alongside Genesis 1–11. Their phrase “three mega rebellions in Genesis 1 through 11,” followed by the links among the snake, the sons of Elohim, the giants, and Babel, shows why the council is not an isolated oddity but part of the Bible’s larger story.",
        "episodeTitle": "Deuteronomy: Moses’ Final Words",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1",
        "durationSec": 4759,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:moses-final-words",
        "startSec": 2049,
        "endSec": 2278,
        "why": "End with the New Testament payoff. The hosts explain Deuteronomy 32 as God giving the nations their inheritance while Yahweh takes Israel as his own, then explicitly connect the pattern to Paul: the Gentiles are “living under the rule of the principalities and powers.” They land the reading in allegiance to Jesus: confessing Jesus as Yahweh means coming out from under “the rebel sons of God’s sway” and giving allegiance to the Creator.",
        "episodeTitle": "Deuteronomy: Moses’ Final Words",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1",
        "durationSec": 4759,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "By the end, the divine council should feel less like a speculative appendix and more like a recurring biblical frame: God is supreme, heavenly beings exercise delegated authority, rebellion distorts that authority, and the New Testament presents Jesus as Lord over the nations and the powers. Keep the passages named in the clips nearby and listen again for the Bible’s shifts between heavenly rulers, earthly nations, and the people gathered to God.",
